.\" LuxOS - manualova stranka lux(1); prikazy musi odpovidat "lux --help" (test_lux_cli.py ji kontroluje)
.TH LUX 1 "2026-10-24" "LuxOS" "LuxOS Manual"
.SH NAME
lux \- LuxOS package manager (repositories, Flathub, community/AUR and .lux apps)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lux
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B lux
(no command: interactive app store in the terminal)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lux
installs, updates and removes software on LuxOS.
Packages from the LuxOS and Arch Linux repositories are handled directly through libalpm
(the package core is the
.B lux-engine
package); applications from the AUR and Flathub and LuxOS
.B .lux
packages are supported as well.
Commands that change the system ask for the administrator password themselves
(sudo in a terminal, a graphical prompt otherwise), so
.B lux
is normally run without sudo.
.PP
There is no
.B pacman
command on LuxOS. Typing a pacman command (for example
.BR "pacman \-Syu" " or " "lux \-S firefox" )
shows the matching
.B lux
command instead.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B (no command)
Interactive app store in the terminal (fzf): search, install, remove and update.
.TP
.BR install ", " i " " \fIpkg\fR...
Install packages. Sources are tried in this order: the LuxOS and Arch Linux repositories,
LuxOS
.B .lux
apps, Flathub (for example
.B lux install zoom
installs us.zoom.Zoom), and last the community source (AUR).
.IP
Community packages are build scripts written by other users and are not checked by LuxOS.
Before building, the script is checked by fixed rules and by LuxAI Lite (when you are signed in
to LuxAI). The package is then built and installed into your
.B sandbox
(luxos\-sandbox), isolated from your files and the system.
It goes into the system only with
.B \-\-system
or when you choose it and type the word
.BR system ;
a dangerous script is never installed into the system and an unchecked one never without your
confirmation. LuxAI never installs community packages.
.TP
.BR remove ", " rm " " \fIpkg\fR...
Remove packages and the dependencies nothing else needs. Flatpak apps (by ID or name) are
removed as well, together with runtimes no app needs any more; their data in ~/.var/app stays.
.TP
.BR update ", " upgrade ", " up
Update everything: the system, community (AUR) packages in the system, Flatpak apps and .lux apps.
New community build scripts are checked again before they are built. Unused Flatpak runtimes are removed.
Configuration steps of other packages run afterwards (/usr/lib/lux/after-update.d).
.TP
.BR check ", " check\-updates
Show available updates without installing them. The package lists are refreshed into a
temporary copy, so this needs no administrator rights and changes nothing on the system.
.TP
.BR search ", " s " " \fIterm\fR
Search the repositories, LuxOS apps, Flathub and the community source (AUR).
.TP
.BI info " pkg"
Show package details.
.TP
.BR list " [" \-\-installed ]
List installed packages.
.TP
.B list \-\-upgradable
List available updates (from the last downloaded package lists; see
.BR check ).
.TP
.BR "list \-\-lux" ", " "list \-\-aur"
List LuxOS (.lux) or AUR packages.
.TP
.BI reinstall " pkg..."
Install the same version again.
.TP
.B autoremove
Remove dependencies nothing needs any more.
.TP
.BI files " pkg"
Files installed by a package.
.TP
.BI owns " file"
Which package owns a file.
.TP
.B clean
Clean the package cache (keeps the number of versions set by
.BR clean\-keep )
and remove unneeded packages.
.TP
.B fix
Repair a locked package database and the signing keys.
.TP
.B configs
Review and merge new configuration files (.luxnew, .luxsave; older .pacnew, .pacsave) left by updates.
.TP
.BR "config check" ", " "config edit"
Check or edit /etc/lux/lux.conf
.RB ( "config show" " prints the effective settings).
.TP
.BR history " [" \fIn\fR "] [" \fIpkg\fR ]
Recent package changes (from /var/log/lux.log).
.TP
.BR pack " " \fIdir\fR " [" \fIout\fR ]
Build a .lux package from a directory with lux.json.
.TP
.B drivers
Detect and install missing drivers.
.TP
.B mirrors
Pick the fastest download mirrors.
.TP
.B doctor
Check system health (services, disk space, configuration, updates).
.TP
.B snapshots
List system snapshots. LuxOS Care takes a read\-only Btrfs snapshot of the system before every
package change (lux, Software Update, Store) and keeps the last 5 plus the last one with the
previous kernel. Each snapshot is in the boot menu as "LuxOS (before update of ...)" (hold Space
while the computer starts); a snapshot started from the menu runs with a temporary writable layer.
.TP
.B snapshot
Take a system snapshot now.
.TP
.BR rollback " [" \fIsnapshot\fR ]
Make a snapshot the system from the next start (asks first). Without a name: the snapshot LuxOS is
running from, otherwise a list to choose from. The current system is kept as a snapshot and
/home is not changed. See
.BR luxos\-care (8)
and "LuxOS Recovery" in the boot menu.
.TP
.BR system " " show | init | edit | check | diff | apply " [" \fIfile\fR ]
The whole system in one LSL file,
.IR /etc/luxos/system.lsl :
packages, Flathub apps, services, users and groups, computer name, language, time zone, keyboard,
LuxOS Care, Lux Guard, firewall and kernel parameters.
.B show
prints a file made from this computer,
.B init
writes it,
.B diff
lists the changes and
.B apply
takes a snapshot, shows the changes, asks and applies them (undo with
.BR "lux rollback" ).
Policies of an organization in
.I /etc/luxos/system.d
are merged with the file;
.B show \-\-origin
shows where each value comes from and
.B locks
the settings the organization has locked.
See
.BR luxos\-system (5).
.TP
.BR domain " " discover | join | leave | status | admins | login | set
Organization network: join a Microsoft Active Directory or Samba AD domain, sign in with domain
accounts (also offline), administrators from domain groups. Needs the package
.BR luxos\-domain .
See
.BR luxos\-domain (8).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask for confirmation.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show all details (and raw errors).
.TP
.B \-\-repo
Only the official repositories.
.TP
.BR \-\-aur ", " \-\-community
Only the community source (AUR).
.TP
.B \-\-system
Install a community package into the system instead of the sandbox (explicit permission).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B /etc/lux/lux.conf
holds the repositories, signature checking, held packages and the settings of
.BR lux .
Each line is
.RI \(dq key " " value \(dq;
lines indented under
.RI \(dq "repo NAME" \(dq
belong to that repository.
After editing run
.BR "lux config check" ,
or use
.B lux config edit
which checks the file right away.
The full format is described in /usr/share/doc/lux/lux.md and in the LuxOS documentation
(/usr/share/luxos/docs, also on https://luxos.lux\-ai.cz/docs/).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/lux/lux.conf
Configuration (repositories, signatures, settings).
.TP
.B /etc/lux/conf.d/*.conf
More configuration in the same format.
.TP
.B /etc/lux/mirrorlist
Arch Linux mirrors (lux mirrors).
.TP
.B ~/.config/lux/lux.conf
Per\-user settings (aur, review\-pkgbuild, flatpak, clean\-keep).
.TP
.B /var/lib/lux/
Package database; installed/ holds the .lux packages.
.TP
.B /var/cache/lux/pkg/
Downloaded packages. A damaged file found there is deleted and downloaded again.
.TP
.B /var/log/lux.log
Log of package changes (lux history).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (for
.BR check :
the check worked, whether or not updates are available).
.TP
.B 1
Error: the message says what failed and what to do.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B lux update
Update the whole system.
.TP
.B lux check
See what an update would install.
.TP
.B lux install firefox vlc
Install two applications.
.TP
.B lux search \-\-aur spotify
Search only the community source (AUR).
.TP
.B lux install zoom
Install Zoom from Flathub.
.TP
.B lux history 50 firefox
The last changes of one package.
.TP
.B lux \-y remove vlc
Remove without asking.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR luxos\-care (8),
.BR luxos\-system (5),
.BR makepkg (8),
.B luxos\-sandbox \-\-help,
.BR flatpak (1),
/usr/share/doc/lux/lux.md
